# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hobbes-leviathan-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.3 have "law_for_parties"; hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "The Judges' Sentences are to be taken by them that plead for Lawes in that particular case" is false. Is S3, "And therefore unlesse there be an Interpreter authorised by the Soveraign, from which the subordinate Judges are not to recede, the Interpreter can be no other than the ordinary Judges, in the some manner, as they are in cases of the unwritten Law; and their Sentences are to be taken by them that plead, for Lawes in that particular case; but not to bind other Judges, in like cases to give like judgements.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.3 have "endless_interpretation → ¬commentator_interpreter" and "authorised_interpreter ∨ commentator_interpreter ∨ judges_interpreter" and "binds_other_judges ∧ judge_may_err → error_changes_law"; hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.2 has "endless_interpretation → ¬commentator_interpreter" and "¬authorised_interpreter ∧ ¬commentator_interpreter → judges_interpreter" and "judges_interpreter → law_for_parties" and "judge_may_err ∧ ¬error_changes_law → ¬binds_other_judges"; they differ when only "There will be no end of such Interpretation", "There is an Interpreter authorised by the Soveraign, from which the subordinate Judges are not to recede", "The ordinary Judges are the Interpreter of written Lawes", "The Judges' Sentences bind other Judges in like cases to give like judgements", "A Judge may erre in the Interpretation even of written Lawes" and "An errour of a subordinate Judge can change the Law, which is the generall Sentence of the Soveraigne" are true, which the first allows
hobbes-leviathan-2.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.3 have "(¬authorised_interpreter → judges_interpreter) ∧ law_for_parties ∧ ¬binds_other_judges"; hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.2 has "¬authorised_interpreter → judges_interpreter ∧ law_for_parties ∧ ¬binds_other_judges"; they differ when only "There is an Interpreter authorised by the Soveraign, from which the subordinate Judges are not to recede", "The ordinary Judges are the Interpreter of written Lawes", "The Judges' Sentences are to be taken by them that plead for Lawes in that particular case" and "The Judges' Sentences bind other Judges in like cases to give like judgements" are true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-2.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2 and hobbes-leviathan-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "There is an Interpreter authorised by the Soveraign, from which the subordinate Judges are not to recede", "The ordinary Judges are the Interpreter of written Lawes", "The Judges' Sentences are to be taken by them that plead for Lawes in that particular case" and "The Judges' Sentences bind other Judges in like cases to give like judgements" are true. Is the conclusion, "And therefore unlesse there be an Interpreter authorised by the Soveraign, from which the subordinate Judges are not to recede, the Interpreter can be no other than the ordinary Judges, in the some manner, as they are in cases of the unwritten Law; and their Sentences are to be taken by them that plead, for Lawes in that particular case; but not to bind other Judges, in like cases to give like judgements.", true then?
